<Book title="Practice · Voltage dividers" practice>
<div class="chapter-hero"><div class="eyebrow"><span>CHAPTER 01</span><span class="hairline"/>THE PRACTICE NOTEBOOK</div><h1>Make the ideas<br/><em>your own.</em></h1><p class="dek">Five problems on divider ratios, loading, and design. Work on paper, use a calculator, and reveal only as much help as you need.</p><div class="chapter-meta"><span>30–45 min</span><span>Independent practice · Fully worked solutions</span></div></div>
<p class="practice-intro">These problems are separate from the chapter’s illustrative figures. Start with the ones that interest you. Assume ideal DC sources and resistors unless stated otherwise, and give voltages relative to the bottom reference node.</p>
<div class="practice-guide"><strong>Before opening a solution:</strong> draw the connections, name the unknown, and estimate what a plausible answer would look like. There is no score or automatic grading in this prototype.</div>

<article class="problem" id="problem-1"><span class="tiny-label">01 / RATIO & CURRENT</span><h2>Same voltage, different cost</h2><p>A 12 V supply drives R₁ = 30 kΩ above the output and R₂ = 10 kΩ below it. No load is connected. Find the output voltage, divider current, and total dissipation. What changes if both resistances are divided by ten?</p>
<details><summary>Show a hint</summary><div><p>Find the total series resistance first. Total power can be found from supply voltage × supply current.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Read the worked solution</summary><div><Equation tex={String.raw`V_{\mathrm{out}}=12\frac{10}{30+10}=3\ \mathrm V,\qquad I=\frac{12}{40\,000}=0.3\ \mathrm{mA}`}/><p>Total dissipation is 12 V × 0.3 mA = <strong>3.6 mW</strong>. R₁ dissipates 2.7 mW and R₂ dissipates 0.9 mW.</p><p>With 3 kΩ and 1 kΩ, the ratio stays one quarter: output remains <strong>3 V</strong>. Current becomes <strong>3 mA</strong> and total dissipation <strong>36 mW</strong>. Output resistance falls from 7.5 kΩ to 750 Ω, so the new divider is less sensitive to a given load.</p></div></details></article>

<article class="problem" id="problem-2"><span class="tiny-label">02 / LOADING</span><h2>The missing volt</h2><p>A 10 V supply feeds a divider with R₁ = R₂ = 10 kΩ. A 20 kΩ load is connected from its midpoint to ground. Find the output voltage and all three branch currents. Check current conservation at the midpoint.</p>
<details><summary>Show a hint</summary><div><p>The original divider has Vₜₕ = 5 V and Rₜₕ = 5 kΩ. After finding the loaded output, use the voltage across each physical resistor to calculate its current.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Read the worked solution</summary><div><Equation tex={String.raw`V_{\mathrm{out}}=5\frac{20}{5+20}=4\ \mathrm V`}/><p>Current through R₁ is (10 − 4) V / 10 kΩ = <strong>0.6 mA</strong>. Through R₂ it is 4 V / 10 kΩ = <strong>0.4 mA</strong>. The load draws 4 V / 20 kΩ = <strong>0.2 mA</strong>.</p><p>At the node, 0.6 mA enters and 0.4 + 0.2 = 0.6 mA leaves. The output is 20% below its unloaded value. Using the unloaded current of 0.5 mA after connecting the load would violate the actual node equation.</p></div></details></article>

<article class="problem" id="problem-3"><span class="tiny-label">03 / DESIGN CONSTRAINT</span><h2>Spend a 2% error budget</h2><p>You need an unloaded 6 V midpoint from 12 V, using two equal resistors. The receiving circuit has a 47 kΩ input resistance to ground. Find the largest nominal value of each resistor that gives at most 2% loading error. Evaluate a choice of 1.8 kΩ for each resistor. Ignore tolerance here.</p>
<details><summary>Show a hint</summary><div><p>For equal resistors R, the output resistance is R/2. Use ε = Rₜₕ / (Rₜₕ + Rₗ), and solve the inequality before choosing a component value.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Read the worked solution</summary><div><Equation tex={String.raw`R_{\mathrm{th}}\leq\frac{0.02}{0.98}(47\ \mathrm{k\Omega})=0.95918\ \mathrm{k\Omega}`}/><p>Therefore each resistor must be no larger than <strong>1.91837 kΩ</strong>. Two 1.8 kΩ resistors give Rₜₕ = 0.9 kΩ.</p><Equation tex={String.raw`\epsilon=\frac{0.9}{47+0.9}=1.879\%,\qquad V_{\mathrm{out}}=6\frac{47}{47.9}=5.8873\ \mathrm V`}/><p>Unloaded current is 12 V / 3.6 kΩ = <strong>3.333 mA</strong>; total unloaded dissipation is <strong>40 mW</strong>. The nominal loading target is met. A final design would also include resistor tolerance, the range of input resistance, supply error, and power margin.</p></div></details></article>

<article class="problem" id="problem-4"><span class="tiny-label">04 / WORST-CASE REASONING</span><h2>Two 1% resistors</h2><p>An exact 12 V supply drives an unloaded divider made from nominal 30 kΩ and 10 kΩ resistors, each with independent ±1% tolerance. Find the minimum and maximum output voltage. Does increasing both resistors by exactly 1% change the ratio?</p>
<details><summary>Show a hint</summary><div><p>The output falls when R₁ increases and rises when R₂ increases. Use opposite tolerance extremes for the worst cases.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Read the worked solution</summary><div><Equation tex={String.raw`V_{\min}=12\frac{9.9}{30.3+9.9}=2.95522\ \mathrm V`}/><Equation tex={String.raw`V_{\max}=12\frac{10.1}{29.7+10.1}=3.04523\ \mathrm V`}/><p>Relative to nominal 3 V, these are approximately −1.493% and +1.508%. The unequal divider does not have the symmetric ±1% voltage bounds of an equal-resistor divider.</p><p>If both resistors increase by exactly 1%, a common factor of 1.01 cancels from numerator and denominator. Output remains exactly <strong>3 V</strong>, while current decreases. Independent tolerance specifies possible individual values; it does not imply equal tracking.</p></div></details></article>

<article class="problem" id="problem-5"><span class="tiny-label">05 / DIAGNOSIS</span><h2>Infer the hidden load</h2><p>An ideal 10 V source drives two exact 10 kΩ resistors. The midpoint measures 5 V with an external module disconnected and 2 V with it connected. Assume the measuring instrument draws negligible current. If the module behaves as a resistance to ground, what is that resistance? Would a disconnected module alone explain a reading of 0 V?</p>
<details><summary>Show a hint</summary><div><p>Use the Thévenin equivalent and rearrange Vₒᵤₜ = VₜₕRₗ / (Rₜₕ + Rₗ) to solve for Rₗ.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>Read the worked solution</summary><div><Equation tex={String.raw`R_L=\frac{V_{\mathrm{out}}R_{\mathrm{th}}}{V_{\mathrm{th}}-V_{\mathrm{out}}}=\frac{2(5\ \mathrm{k\Omega})}{5-2}=3.333\ \mathrm{k\Omega}`}/><p>The module draws 2 V / 3.333 kΩ = <strong>0.6 mA</strong>. R₂ carries 0.2 mA, while R₁ supplies 0.8 mA. The currents balance.</p><p>A disconnected module leaves the original unloaded divider, so it predicts <strong>5 V</strong>, not 0 V. A short to ground, missing supply, open R₁, or measurement/reference error could explain a zero reading under different fault assumptions. One observation cannot uniquely identify the fault.</p></div></details></article>
